  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 334 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nMid to upper level ridging continues today, keeping the heat and \nhumidity in place. Upper troughing to the NE may start to nudge \nsouthwestward into the area late today, weakening the subsidence \naloft. This, plus expected upstream convection, could bring isolated \nto scattered showers and storms into the northern part of the \nforecast area. However, confidence on how far south the line of \nconvection gets remains low thus confidence is low on precipitation \nfor central Indiana. Should these storms reach central Indiana, \ncan't rule out isolated severe in the form of brief damaging \nwinds or hail, but this threat is quite conditional. Timing on the\nstorms also comes with low confidence and will likely depend on \nsmall scale features. Isolated storms could pop up as early as \nthis afternoon but most of the latest runs are leaning more so \ntowards Friday morning convection. Chance of precipitation \ncontinues into Saturday. \n\nHeat will continue into the start of the weekend and a lack of sky \ncover and convection will only keep the heat around that much \nlonger. Highs are expected to remain in the 90s through Friday and \neven into Saturday for much of the area with max heat indices near \nto above 105. \n\nThe ridge axis is still expected to retrograde into the weekend \nwhich could allow for a brief backdoor cold front and temperatures \nnearer to normal for the start of the new week with dry weather \nlikely during this time. Another warming trend looks possible for\nthe end of the period with a system possibly bringing additional \nrain chances for the middle to end of next week.",
